FAQ = [
 ("What is the best aftermarket hitch for a 2022–2026 Ford Maverick?",
  "For most owners, the CURT 13504. It is a Class 3 hitch with a 2 in receiver, rated 4,000 lb gross trailer weight and 600 lb tongue weight, weighs 33 lb, bolts on with no drilling and carries a limited lifetime warranty. etrailer sells it as C84BR for $265.14. If you want the most rated capacity on paper, the Draw-Tite 76557 and Reese 84557 are rated 4,500 lb and 675 lb, but your Maverick's own tow rating is the real limit either way."),
 ("How much can a Ford Maverick tow with an aftermarket hitch?",
  "The same as without one. Ford rates every Maverick at 2,000 lb unless it left the factory with the 4K Tow Package (option code 53Q), which raises AWD trucks to 4,000 lb. Bolting on a hitch rated 4,000 or 4,500 lb does not change the truck's cooling, brakes or rating. The lower of the two numbers always wins, so a non-4K Maverick with a Draw-Tite 76557 is still a 2,000 lb tow vehicle."),
 ("Can I add the 4K Tow Package to my Maverick after purchase?",
  "Not in any practical sense. The package is a factory option that bundles a Class III receiver and 4-/7-pin connector with a trailer brake controller, an upgraded cooling fan, a transmission oil cooler on hybrids and a higher-capacity radiator on EcoBoost trucks. Go-Parts' Maverick guide describes these as factory-only upgrades. An aftermarket hitch gives you the receiver, not the cooling hardware that earns the 4,000 lb rating."),
 ("Does my Maverick already have a hitch?",
  "Look under the rear bumper. If there is a square 2 in receiver tube and a 4-/7-pin connector, your truck almost certainly has the 4K Tow Package and you don't need anything on this page except perhaps a ball mount. CURT lists the 13504 as fitting 2022–2026 Mavericks excluding factory receiver, and the aftermarket hitches here bolt to the same frame area, so they are for trucks that came without one."),
 ("Can the Maverick Hybrid tow 4,000 lb?",
  "Only from the 2025 model year, and only with AWD and the 4K Tow Package. Ford's 2025 towing guide lists the 2.5L hybrid at 2,000 lb in FWD form with or without the package, and 2,000 lb or 4,000 lb in AWD form depending on whether the package is fitted. For 2022–2024, the 4K package was offered only with the 2.0L EcoBoost and AWD, so every 2022–2024 hybrid is a 2,000 lb truck."),
 ("Which wiring harness does a Maverick need?",
  "For a 4-way flat plug, CURT's custom harnesses plug in at the taillight with no splicing. The CURT 56477 fits 2022–2027 Mavericks but excludes 2022–2024 hybrids, and on 2025–2027 trucks it needs the LED taillight accent. The CURT 56567 covers 2025–2026 trucks without that accent. If you have a 2022–2024 hybrid, confirm your harness with the seller before ordering, because the common 56477 is not listed for it."),
 ("Do I have to cut the bumper to install a hitch on a Maverick?",
  "Usually a little. etrailer's install notes for the CURT 13504 say fascia trimming is required, with a choice between a small cutout and removing a larger panel section, and that an exhaust hanger may need to come off. Its Blue Ox and etrailer-brand Maverick hitches list the same kind of fascia and underbody trimming. None of them require drilling into the frame. Plan on a utility knife and about an hour."),
 ("Will a hitch interfere with the Maverick's spare tire?",
  "etrailer's product notes for the CURT C84BR (13504) say it is compatible with full-size spare configurations, which matters because the 4K Tow Package adds a 17 in conventional spare according to Jalopnik. For the other hitches here, the makers don't publish a spare-tire note, so confirm on the listing if you have swapped to a larger spare. The spare hangs under the bed, ahead of where the hitch cross tube sits."),
 ("Can I use a hitch bike rack or cargo carrier on a 2,000 lb Maverick?",
  "Yes, and that is what most non-4K owners use a hitch for. What matters is tongue weight, not trailer weight. Ford lists a 400 lb maximum tongue load for the Maverick, and every hitch here is rated at 600 lb or more, so the rack plus bikes or cargo must stay under Ford's 400 lb figure and under the rack's own rating. A 2 in receiver also takes more racks than a 1.25 in one."),
 ("Is a Class 3 hitch overkill on a small truck like the Maverick?",
  "No. Class 3 simply means a 2 in receiver and a rating the maker has tested to. Every brand-name bolt-on hitch for the Maverick is Class 3, and the 2 in receiver is what Ford itself fits with the 4K package. The extra rating over your truck's limit is margin, not permission to tow more. You still tow to 2,000 lb or 4,000 lb depending on how your truck was built."),
]

